Import "../Physics.lingua"

Solve charge redistribution and account for the dissipated energy (@physics-charge-sharing: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Ask-which-electrical-quantities-an-observation-identifies.lingua#r_00248BCB328E2A41569B7E86C8") { r_00AA29526C7EBA203EF2EA6FB9
Use the two-capacitor system defined in [State the physical assumptions behind a lumped circuit](State-the-physical-assumptions-behind-a-lumped-circuit.lingua#r_001A7088BC509FE999669C908D). Let initial voltages be a and b; define CΣ=C₁+C₂, C*=C₁C₂/CΣ, conserved upper charge QΣ=C₁a+C₂b and final common voltage v_f=QΣ/CΣ. Set d=v₁−v₂. Subtracting the charge equations gives d′=−d/(RC*), so with τ=RC*>0 the solution is d(t)=(a−b)e^(−t/τ). The exponential and its derivative are shared preparation in [Construct a logarithm from accumulated reciprocal change](../../chemistry/core/Construct-a-logarithm-from-accumulated-reciprocal-change.lingua#r_048F416B6E95AE137A181635B7) and [Define exponential change by an inverse and prove its rate law](../../chemistry/core/Define-exponential-change-by-an-inverse-and-prove-its-rate-law.lingua#r_00708D2CC09F8E0AA4DEC25359); their existing continuations need not be changed.

Recover each voltage by solving the two simultaneous equations C₁v₁+C₂v₂=QΣ and v₁−v₂=d: v₁=v_f+(C₂/CΣ)d and v₂=v_f−(C₁/CΣ)d. These satisfy both initial values and the original differential equations. Uniqueness follows without a general ODE theorem: multiplying the difference equation for two candidate d solutions by e^(t/τ) gives derivative zero, and the same initial value makes their difference zero. The charge constraint then fixes both voltages.

The current is I=d/R. The signed charge transferred from node 1 to node 2 by time t is C*(a−b)(1−e^(−t/τ)), obtained either by integrating I or by comparing C₁a with C₁v₁. Negative a−b simply reverses the transfer. Voltage is continuous in this finite positive-R model; current can begin nonzero. The ideal abrupt connection ignores the earliest propagation and parasitic effects. No conclusion about an actual switching waveform is supplied by that assumption.

Expand the stored energy U=(C₁v₁²+C₂v₂²)/2 using the expressions above. The cross terms cancel, giving U=U_f+C*d²/2, where U_f=CΣv_f²/2. The excess over U_f decays as e^(−2t/τ). Direct integration of resistor power yields ∫₀ᵗI²R dt=C*(a−b)²(1−e^(−2t/τ))/2=U(0)−U(t). Both charge conservation and this energy identity must hold. Conservation of charge does not imply conservation of the capacitors' stored energy alone: the resistor receives the difference.

For C₁=2 μF, C₂=3 μF, R=5 kΩ, a=5 V and b=0 V, τ=6 ms and v_f=2 V. Initially the upper-node charge is 10 μC and stored energy 25 μJ. The final stored energy is 10 μJ and resistor energy 15 μJ. At t=τ ln 2, the decay factor is exactly 1/2: v₁=3.5 V, v₂=1 V, I=0.5 mA, transferred charge 3 μC, stored energy 13.75 μJ and dissipated energy 11.25 μJ. All values follow from exact fractions; a decimal approximation to ln 2 is unnecessary for this state.

**Work:** derive the energy decomposition and the integral independently. Compare a=b, a<b and a case with QΣ=0 but a≠b. The latter ends at zero voltage while still having positive initial energy. Explain why varying positive R changes the time scale but not the eventual energy loss under these premises. Setting R=0 is a singular change that removes the differential model; it does not prove real redistribution has no dissipation, radiation or magnetic storage. Read [University Physics Volume 2 — OpenStax](../electromagnetism/sources/University-Physics-Volume-2.lingua#r_5JQ0CPQ0RNJNVH175Y9V305J5Q) 10.5 for the related one-capacitor RC derivation, then derive this different coupled system yourself. The full RL/RLC formation in [RC, RL and RLC transients and energy](../electromagnetism/currents/RC-RL-RLC-transients-and-energy.lingua#r_4F9B677SF6134C9JSNNYZ94QCJ) remains contextual later reading.

